# The Book of Motoi

The other books teach you *pieces*. This one hands you the whole
machine.

Motoi is a **fantasy console** — a small, made-up computer that lives
inside a real one. It has a screen, a sound chip, a set of buttons, a
clock that ticks, and a language you talk to it with. That language is
Motoi Scheme. If you have read the Book of Scheme, you already know how
to speak. This book shows you the room you are speaking *into*.

If you grew up near a PICO-8 or a TIC-80, you know the shape of the
thing: you turn it on, a splash screen greets you, and you are dropped
somewhere you can immediately make a pixel move. Motoi is built in that
tradition. It boots to a splash with **four doors**:

- **IDE** — the REPL. A blinking prompt that runs whatever you type.
- **Music** — a composer for beats, notes, and the synth.
- **Animation** — a composer for tweens, easing, and frames.
- **Game** — a composer for sprites, input, and the tick loop.

Every door opens onto the same console underneath. The synth the Music
door plays is the same synth a game beeps through. The sprite the Game
door moves is the same sprite the Animation door tweens. There is one
machine. The four doors are just four good places to start.

## What a fantasy console is (and is not)

A fantasy console is a computer with **limits on purpose**. A real
computer will let you do anything, which is another way of saying it
will let you get lost. A fantasy console draws a small box — this many
pixels, this many sound channels, this one language — and inside that
box everything is knowable. You can hold the whole machine in your
head. That is the point. The constraint is the feature.

Motoi's box:

- **One screen.** A framebuffer you draw into, pixel by pixel or sprite
  by sprite.
- **One clock.** A tick loop. The world takes a `step`, everything
  moves a little, the screen redraws. Do that sixty times a second and
  you have motion.
- **One sound chip.** A small synth. Notes, beats, a beep on impact.
- **One set of hands.** The buttons a player presses.
- **One memory.** Cortex — the console remembers things you tell it,
  and can be asked about them later.
- **One language.** Motoi Scheme. Nothing you build needs a second
  language to build it.

What Motoi is *not*: it is not a game engine you configure, not an app
you fill in blanks on, not a toy that does one thing. It is a computer.
You program it. The difference between a toy and a console is that a
console has a language, and the language is how you find out what the
console can really do.

## How this book is shaped

Sixteen chapters, read in order. Each one turns on one more part of the
machine and gives you a runnable program that uses it.

1. **Boot the console.** The splash, the four doors, your first prompt.
2. **The REPL.** Talking to Motoi. Expressions in, values out.
3. **The screen.** The framebuffer. Putting a pixel where you want it.
4. **The sprite.** A thing on the screen with a position you can read
   and write.
5. **The tick.** The loop that makes still things move. `world/step`.
6. **The buttons.** Input. Letting a player push your sprite around.
7. **Motion.** Velocity and gravity. Making the sprite move *itself*.
8. **Sound.** The synth. Your first beep, your first note.
9. **The beat.** Transport and tempo. Sound that keeps time.
10. **Animation.** Tweens and easing. Motion that has *taste*.
11. **Collision.** Two sprites noticing each other.
12. **The scene.** A screen full of things, composed on purpose.
13. **Make a game.** The loop, the input, the collision — one small
    playable thing.
14. **Make music.** The synth, the beat, the sequence — one small
    song.
15. **The console remembers.** Cortex. Giving the machine a memory and
    asking it questions.
16. **Ship it.** Saving your creation as a cart. Sharing the machine
    state so someone else can press play.

Then eight appendices — the reference shelf you reach for once you know
your way around: the boot splash map, every console verb by namespace,
the framebuffer model, the tick/scheduler model, the synth internals,
the cart format, the controls reference, and a troubleshooting guide.

## How to read it

Keep the console open. This is not a book you read on a train away from
the machine — every chapter has a program, and every program *runs*.

- Read the program.
- Guess what it will do.
- Type it into the IDE door and run it.
- Change one number.
- Run it again and watch the machine answer differently.

Guessing is the exercise. Running is the check. The machine never lies
to you, which is the best thing a teacher can offer.

> **Warning**
>
> Every fenced program here is a full, runnable program — and a
> training pair. The programs on the page and the programs in the
> corpus are the same programs. If you skim past one, you skim past the
> point of the book.

> **Tip**
>
> If a program errors, that is not a failure — it is the console
> telling you something true about itself. Appendix H is the
> troubleshooting shelf. Read the error, not around it.

## Where this sits among the books

- [[book-of-scheme]] — the language itself, learned slowly. Read this
  first if the parentheses are still strange.
- [[book-of-motion]] — the numeric feel of movement, deeper than
  Chapter 7 goes.
- [[book-of-animation]] — the drawing craft over the motion layer.
- [[book-of-sound]] · [[book-of-music]] — the synth and the beat,
  deeper than Chapters 8 and 9.
- [[book-of-arcade-games]] · [[book-of-simulation-games]] ·
  [[book-of-narrative-games]] — whole games, once Chapter 13 has shown
  you the loop.
- [[book-of-introspection]] — how the console sees itself, once
  Chapter 15 has shown you Cortex.

This book is the **map of the room**. The other books are the deep
tours of each corner. Read this one to know where the corners are.

---

*Turn the page. We boot the console.*
